WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.780
Support types northwind bikes into the shop search.

2
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.559
The search index is split over three machines.

3
00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:13.708
When we look up one shop by its id, we know exactly which machine to ask.

4
00:00:13.708 --> 00:00:15.265
But a word isn't an id.

5
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:18.529
So how does the search find the ten best shops?

6
00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:22.504
And why won't most sites let you jump straight to page 1,000?

7
00:00:22.504 --> 00:00:23.807
Hold both questions.

8
00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:29.087
Picture an encyclopedia in three volumes, each with its own index at the back.

9
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:35.348
To find every page about bicycles, you open all three indexes and put the pages together.

10
00:00:35.348 --> 00:00:38.337
No volume can tell you what the others hold.

11
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:43.511
And a careful librarian keeps a photocopy of every volume on a different shelf.

12
00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:47.327
OpenSearch, a free search program, works the same way.

13
00:00:47.327 --> 00:00:50.756
Each volume is a shard, a piece of the search index.

14
00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:57.916
Its index at the back is the shard's inverted index, a list of every word with the documents that contain it.

15
00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:03.239
And each photocopy is a replica, a copy of the shard kept on another machine.

16
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:04.854
First, a write.

17
00:01:04.854 --> 00:01:11.535
A new shop, M-0054, can arrive at any node, which coordinates the request.

18
00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:14.658
It hashes the id, and the hash says shard 2.

19
00:01:14.658 --> 00:01:20.109
So it sends the shop to shard 2's primary copy, which passes it on to the replica.

20
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.314
Only when both have it does the answer come back: total 2, successful 2.

21
00:01:25.314 --> 00:01:28.377
One shard, two copies, nobody else bothered.

22
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:29.602
Now the search.

23
00:01:29.602 --> 00:01:34.030
You might think it works the same way: find the right shard and ask it.

24
00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.025
But which shard holds northwind?

25
00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:41.810
Each shard's index covers only its own shops, like the index at the back of one volume.

26
00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.951
No shard can say which other shards hold matches.

27
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:47.347
So the coordinator asks them all.

28
00:01:47.347 --> 00:01:55.195
It sends the query to one copy of every shard, primary or replica, picking the copy that has answered fastest lately.

29
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.112
Here's the trick that keeps it cheap.

30
00:01:58.112 --> 00:02:08.666
In the first round, each shard scores its own shops with BM25, the ranking formula, and sends back only ids and scores, for the best few it has.

31
00:02:08.666 --> 00:02:09.912
No documents yet.

32
00:02:09.912 --> 00:02:11.597
That's the query phase.

33
00:02:11.767 --> 00:02:15.357
The coordinator merges those short lists into one ranking.

34
00:02:15.357 --> 00:02:21.115
Then the second round asks for the full documents of the page it will show, and nothing else.

35
00:02:21.115 --> 00:02:22.539
That's the fetch phase.

36
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:23.890
Query, then fetch.

37
00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:29.775
Like jotting page numbers from each volume's index before you carry any book to the desk.

38
00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:36.682
Now ask for a deep page: skip 9,990 hits and show 10, on an index with five shards.

39
00:02:36.682 --> 00:02:40.526
How many hits does the coordinator have to sort?

40
00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:41.808
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:45.553
50,000.

42
00:02:45.553 --> 00:02:57.003
No shard knows where its hits fall in the overall order, so each must send its own top 10,000, and the coordinator sorts all 50,000 to return 10.

43
00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:03.052
That's five librarians each copying out ten thousand page numbers so you can read the last ten.

44
00:03:03.333 --> 00:03:09.899
So OpenSearch stops skip plus size at 10,000 by default, with a setting called max result window.

45
00:03:09.899 --> 00:03:14.975
To go deeper, search after carries on from the last hit of the page before.

46
00:03:14.975 --> 00:03:17.817
And that's why page 1,000 is out of reach.

47
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.520
There's a second cost.

48
00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.167
A search is as slow as its slowest shard.

49
00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:26.445
Say each shard answers in time 99% of the time.

50
00:03:26.445 --> 00:03:34.689
Then all 10 shards of a search answer in time only 0.99 to the power 10, about 90% of the time.

51
00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.363
One more surprise.

52
00:03:36.363 --> 00:03:42.295
BM25 counts how rare a word is, and each shard counts only its own shops.

53
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.207
With millions per shard, they agree.

54
00:03:45.207 --> 00:03:47.237
With a handful, they don't.

55
00:03:47.237 --> 00:03:51.523
In the lesson's lab, the plain search ranks M-0045 first.

56
00:03:51.700 --> 00:04:03.205
Ask for D F S query then fetch, which adds a first round to collect those counts from every shard, and M-0042, which has both words, comes first again.

57
00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:09.937
So the moral of the story: a search asks every shard, and more shards add work to every search.

58
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.723
One quick check.

59
00:04:11.723 --> 00:04:15.339
Support fetches shop M-0054 by its id.

60
00:04:15.339 --> 00:04:17.813
How many shards get asked?

61
00:04:17.813 --> 00:04:19.336
Pause and think.

62
00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:23.219
Just one.

63
00:04:23.219 --> 00:04:26.990
The id hashes to shard 2, so it goes straight there.

64
00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.819
Words make you ask everyone; ids don't.
